y, remembered as ruler of the kingdom of Pengwern in the area of modern Shropshire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cynegils"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cynegils.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cynegils</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Cynegils was king of the West Saxons from about 611 to about 642.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/cynewulf-of-wessex"><span class="enc-name">Cynewulf of Wessex</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Cynewulf was king of Wessex from 757 to 786, coming to power after the deposition of Sigeberht.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/cyngen-ap-cadell"><span class="enc-name">Cyngen ap Cadell</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Cyngen ap Cadell was the last king of Powys of the old royal line, ruling from about 808 until 854.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cynllaith"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cynllaith.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cynllaith</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Cynllaith was a medieval commote in north-east Wales, in the valley of the River Cynllaith on the borders of modern Powys and Wrexham.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cynric"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/cynric.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cynric</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Cynric is named by the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle as the son, or in some sources the grandson, of Cerdic and his successor as king of Wessex, ruling from 534 to 560.</span></a></li></ul>
